<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reamker</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Reamker is the Cambodian version of the Indian epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, telling the story of Preah Ream (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>) and his rescue of his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a> from the demon king Krong Reap. Known in written form from the 16th and 17th centuries, it was adapted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> Buddhist values and is performed in classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> dance and shadow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. Its scenes are painted on the gallery walls of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> Pagoda in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reamker.jpg" alt="Reamker" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcin Konsek · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2016_Phnom_Penh,_Pa%C5%82ac_Kr%C3%B3lewski,_Malowid%C5%82a_przedstawiaj%C4%85ce_sceny_z_Reamker_(27).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
